>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="fix">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do I thaw a frozen condensate pipe?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Pour warm water slowly along the plastic pipe outside, then reset the boiler. It usually takes 10 to 20 minutes.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Find the pipe.</strong> Look for a white or grey plastic pipe coming out of the wall near the boiler, running down to a drain outside.</li>
        <li><strong>Fill a kettle or jug with warm water.</strong> Warm, like washing-up water. Not boiling.</li>
        <li><strong>Pour it slowly along the pipe.</strong> Start at the open end and work back towards the wall. Spend longer on bends, where ice builds up.</li>
        <li><strong>Or hold a hot water bottle on it.</strong> This works well on bends and where the pipe is hard to reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Reset the boiler.</strong> Press the reset button once. Your boiler manual shows where it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Wait a few minutes.</strong> The boiler should fire up and the gurgling should stop.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Never use boiling water. Do not climb a ladder in ice or in the dark. Do not take the pipe apart. If the pipe is high up or you cannot reach it, call us.</p></div>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Tried it and the boiler still will not start?</strong> We can talk you through it, or come out 24/7.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is it the condensate pipe, or something else?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If it is below freezing, the boiler gurgles and then stops with a fault code, it is very likely the condensate pipe.</p>
      <p>A modern condensing boiler makes water as it runs. That water drains away through the condensate pipe. When the outside part of the pipe freezes, the water backs up and the boiler shuts down to protect itself.</p>
      <h3>Common signs</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>It is below freezing, or has been overnight</li>
        <li>A gurgling noise from the boiler</li>
        <li>The boiler stops and shows a fault code</li>
        <li>Ice at the open end of the pipe outside</li>
        <li>It works after a reset, then stops again</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Error codes it often shows</h3>
      <p>A frozen condensate pipe usually shows as a lighting or lockout fault, for example Baxi E133, Ideal L2, Worcester Bosch EA 227, or Vaillant F28 or F29. These codes can mean other faults too. See our <a href="/boiler-repair-liverpool#error-codes">boiler error codes</a> for your brand.</p>
      <h3>Check these first</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Does your gas hob work? If not, the gas may be off, or a prepayment meter may be out of credit.</li>
        <li>Is the pressure gauge between 1 and 1.5 bar? If it is low, see <a href="/boiler-losing-pressure-liverpool">boiler losing pressure</a>.</li>
        <li>Is the switch next to the boiler on?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="call">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>When should I call you?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Call us if thawing and one reset have not worked, or if you cannot safely reach the pipe.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>You have thawed the pipe and reset, and it still locks out</li>
        <li>The pipe is high up, on a flat roof, or hard to reach</li>
        <li>The pipe runs inside the wall or under the floor</li>
        <li>It freezes every winter</li>
        <li>You are not sure it is the condensate pipe</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Smell gas?</strong> That is not a condensate problem. Leave the house and call the National Gas Emergency line on 0800 111 999 now.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="prevent">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do I stop it freezing again?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Keep the outside run short, wide and insulated. We can do this for you while we are there.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Lag the outside pipe</strong> with waterproof pipe insulation</li>
        <li><strong>Fit a wider pipe outside.</strong> A wider pipe takes much longer to freeze solid</li>
        <li><strong>Shorten the outside run</strong> so less pipe is in the cold</li>
        <li><strong>Re-route it inside</strong> to a sink or washing machine waste, where it cannot freeze</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In a cold snap, you can also leave the heating on low through the night. A boiler that keeps running is less likely to let the pipe freeze.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does it cost?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The first hour is £120 on weekdays, 9am to 4pm. There is no call-out fee. Thawing and resetting usually fits inside the first hour.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>First hour, inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Weekdays, 9am to 4pm</strong></td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Evenings, nights and weekends</strong></td><td>£150</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bank holidays</strong></td><td>£180</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If the pipe needs lagging, upsizing or re-routing, we tell you the price before we start. See our full <a href="/plumber-prices-liverpool">price list</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
